The writing history below describes the **original October release**; its draft, critiques, response and R1 remain preserved.


[Seven essays](../README.md) · [Current selection](../../../docs/selection.md) · [After the midterm](../../../docs/post-midterm.md)

A public authority trials a new system, then makes essential services so dependent on it that rejection would leave people without care. Continuing the policy may be necessary by then. The essay asks what implementers owed earlier, when they could still preserve a workable way to end the trial.

[Original release R1](R1.md) · [Original draft](draft.md) · [Argument critique](critique-A.md) · [Literature critique](critique-B.md) · [Author response](author-response.md)

## What changed

The revision distinguishes a promise to report, permission to reconsider, and a mandate that reserves a practical power to discontinue. Its duty applies to the third: implementers must make proportionate preparations that let a later public decision take effect. The argument still needs assessment against earlier theories, the legitimacy of the mandate, and the allocation of transition costs.

## Production record

A free investigation begun before the resource pause, completed through the [shared author–two-critics–revision cycle](../README.md#the-shared-writing-cycle). Both critics read the preserved original draft; a fresh editor produced the response and R1. One interrupted critic resumed in the same context after specific authorization. The quota failure and initial authorization mistake remain recorded.

**Recorded settings:** Astra/max draft; GPT-6.1 Sol/high critics; Sol/xhigh editor; Standard continuation.

